  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another cold front will cross the area late today \nand tonight with a few showers, followed by notably below normal \ntemperatures Saturday.\n\nA compact but vigorous closed low will quickly dive southeast across \nQuebec today...with this feature reaching the Upper Saint Lawrence \nValley by early this evening before making its way further \nsoutheastward across New England tonight...then out to sea on \nSaturday. While the deepest moisture and best forcing attendant to \nthis system will pass by to our northeast...our region will still be \nsubject to the passage of an associated trailing cold front later \nthis afternoon and evening. \n\nAs it has looked for the past several days...the best potential for \nshowers in our area will lie across the North Country...which will \nbe closest to the track of the low and consequently where the best \ncombination of moisture and forcing in our area will be realized. \nExpect a few showers to develop there during the early to mid \nafternoon hours...become a bit more numerous late today into this \nevening with the passage of the cold front...and then diminish \novernight as the system departs off to our southeast. Meanwhile \nfurther to the west...both forcing and moisture along the front will \ndiminish with increasing westward extent...and should thus yield \nonly a few short-lived scattered to widely scattered showers across \nthe remainder of the area late this afternoon and evening. \n\nAny leftover showers east of Lake Ontario should come to an end \nearly Saturday morning...with dry weather otherwise expected on \nSaturday as expansive Canadian high pressure and drier air/ \nsubsidence builds southeastward across the Great Lakes. With a much \ncooler airmass in place (850 mb temps of around 0C to +3C Saturday \nmorning, then warming only slightly in the afternoon)...high temps \non Saturday will be notably below normal...with readings largely \npeaking in the lower 60s across the lower elevations...and only in \nthe upper 50s across the higher terrain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Near to slightly below normal temperatures through \nthe first half of next week, with a few showers possible at times \neast of Lake Ontario.\n\nDuring the first half of next week a large...high-amplitude trough \ndraped from New England/the Canadian Maritimes southward across the \nwestern Atlantic will gradually evolve into a closed low somewhere \noff the mid-Atlantic coastline...while a pronounced Omega Block over \ncentral Canada weakens and devolves into broader/flatter ridging \nover the western/central Great Lakes. During this time our region \nwill remain embedded within the broad northerly flow in between \nthese two features...with the latter resulting in an extended period \nof near to slightly below (but quite comfortable) temperatures. At \nthe same time...periodic shortwave impulses dropping through the \nbackside of the larger-scale mean trough will bring the potential \nfor a few showers to the North Country and perhaps the Finger Lakes \nat times...with mainly dry weather favored further west across far \nwestern New York. Later on in the week the closed low should slowly \neject northeastward into the Canadian Maritimes while the broad/flat \nridge to our west builds eastward...with the latter likely resulting \nin a warming trend.",
